{"title":"2020 CEMC CTMC Problems/Individual Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_CEMC_CTMC_Problems/Individual_Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>How many perfect squares greater than 1 are divisors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/a/cead1bf0d99245ad4efa215eea7301577ed6cc4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"27\" height=\"17\" />?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_CEMC_CTMC_Problems/Individual_Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/302e99c1c7cd91a6ae371139e5142ccef5977dca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/1/ee1e74603ac508ba54c1f8bc628d30cc4f5a6412.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2} \\times 3 \\times 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"81\" height=\"18\" />, so the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/8/1e808b9048b2c4de998e0df22b5af02836be9d70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"27\" height=\"17\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/8/4e860958cbc8cde29b667ecd2ec031a1b053c0a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^{2} \\times 3 \\times 5)^{5} = 2^{10} \\times 3^{5} \\times 5^{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"228\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>For a number to be a perfect square, the powers in its prime factorization must all be even. Since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> possible numbers for the power for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />) in the factor, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> for the power for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />), and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> for the power for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/2/70242fdaeef072f45c64150b08b0b25a06e636e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 \\times 3 \\times 3 = 18 \\times 3 = 54$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"190\" height=\"15\" /> possible factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/8/1e808b9048b2c4de998e0df22b5af02836be9d70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"27\" height=\"17\" /> that are perfect squares.\n</p><p>However, we want to subtract <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> because the problem is only looking for factors that are greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/1/11157d57e555af9c9655981078c4e8254a69287f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{0} \\times 3^{0} \\times 5^{0} = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" /> is a perfect square. This means that we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/3/1833e57ba1442553d09fae41034c95c8f4b80e64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{53}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"31\" height=\"25\" /> factors.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_CEMC_CTMC_Problems/Individual_Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/S5hkxMlBdAs?si=ht3qVSZPH0c7EFoM&amp;t=385\">https://youtu.be/S5hkxMlBdAs?si=ht3qVSZPH0c7EFoM&amp;t=385</a>\n</p><p>~CanadaMath (also includes the rest of the Individual Problems)\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_CEMC_CTMC\" title=\"2020 CEMC CTMC\">2020 CEMC CTMC</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_CEMC_CTMC_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2020 CEMC CTMC Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_CEMC_CTMC_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2020 CEMC CTMC Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_